menu "Serial MUX (COBS channel-framing serial multiplexer)"

config MCUX_COMPONENT_component.serial_mux
    bool "Serial MUX COBS core"
    help
      Portable COBS channel-framing core that multiplexes several logical
      byte streams (channels) onto one physical UART. Wire framing is
      identical to the PC-side scripts: COBS(<channel_byte> + <payload>) 0x00.
      Arch-neutral with no hardware dependencies. Enabling this option
      auto-enables the slot IPC, hub, client, and transport sub-parts
      (each defaults to y inside this component); disable any of them
      explicitly if a build does not need that role.

if MCUX_COMPONENT_component.serial_mux

config MCUX_COMPONENT_component.serial_mux.slots
    bool "Shared-memory SPSC slot IPC"
    default y
    help
      Lock-free single-producer / single-consumer slot rings in a shared
      memory region used to move payloads between the hub core (which owns
      the physical UART) and non-hub cores. Required by both the hub and
      client roles on a multicore SoC.

config MCUX_COMPONENT_component.serial_mux.hub
    bool "Hub role (owns physical UART, COBS encode/decode + routing)"
    default y
    select MCUX_COMPONENT_component.serial_mux.slots
    help
      Runs on the core that owns the physical UART. Performs COBS
      encode/decode on the wire and routes decoded payloads to/from the
      per-channel shared-memory slots.

config MCUX_COMPONENT_component.serial_mux.client
    bool "Client role (non-hub core, no UART access)"
    default y
    select MCUX_COMPONENT_component.serial_mux.slots
    help
      Runs on a non-hub core. Exchanges payloads with the hub through the
      shared-memory slots; never touches the UART. The channel id is
      implied by the core's 0-based index.

config MUX_CLIENT_BLOCKING_RECV
    bool "Client recv blocks until a byte arrives (console getchar semantics)"
    depends on MCUX_COMPONENT_component.serial_mux.client
    default y
    help
      No prefix in generated macro
      Selects blocking vs non-blocking client downlink receive. When enabled
      (default), MUX_CLIENT_RecvBlocking() spins until at least one downlink
      byte is available, and a console reader on top of the client (e.g. the
      muxuart HAL UART adapter) uses it so GETCHAR blocks like a real UART
      instead of returning a no-data sentinel (which an echo loop would
      otherwise stream back to the PC). Disable to keep the original
      non-blocking recv, which returns immediately when no byte is pending
      (poll-style callers).


config MCUX_COMPONENT_component.serial_mux.transport
    bool "LPUART transport + one-call hub/client bring-up"
    default y
    select MCUX_COMPONENT_component.serial_mux.slots
    help
      Reusable application glue on top of the hub/client roles: a hub-side
      LPUART software TX/RX ring pair with a drain-on-TDRE service call and an
      RX ISR body and a deadlock-free channel self-console emit. Lets a demo
      bring up a single-UART console mux by only supplying board wiring, with
      no ring/ISR boilerplate.
      The hub-side and client-side sources are compiled per role (driven by the
      serial_mux.hub / serial_mux.client options).

# Serial MUX hub transport backends. This is an INDEPENDENT bool group (not a
# single-select choice), so more backends (e.g. I2C, SPI) can be added later as
# their own MUX_TRANSPORT_* options and enabled alongside LPUART. Each option
# compiles one concrete mux_transport_t backend source. The mux core stays
# transport-neutral: a hub instance is bound to exactly one physical link, so
# enabling several backends lets an application create one hub instance per link
# and pass each a different backend - this is how multiple backends coexist at
# runtime. For now only the LPUART backend exists. Leaving it disabled is the
# "custom" case: no in-tree backend is compiled and the application supplies its
# own mux_transport_t and drives MUX_HUB_Init() with that backend's write/read
# hooks directly.
config MUX_TRANSPORT_LPUART
    bool "LPUART transport backend"
    depends on MCUX_COMPONENT_component.serial_mux.transport
    default y
    help
      No prefix in generated macro
      Carry the COBS wire stream over an LPUART instance (software TX/RX rings
      plus an RX-full ISR). This is the default in-tree backend. Board wiring
      (LPUART instance, IRQ, clock) is supplied via mux_transport_lpuart_cfg_t /
      the MUX_UART_Hub* one-call bring-up.

config MUX_SLOTS_CORES
    int "Number of non-hub client cores served by the hub"
    depends on MCUX_COMPONENT_component.serial_mux.slots
    help
      No prefix in generated macro
      type unsigned
      Non-hub cores that get an uplink/downlink SPSC ring pair (and a
      logical channel) in the shared slot region. Mandatory: there is
      deliberately no default; set CONFIG_MUX_SLOTS_CORES in the
      application or the build fails. On imx943evk set it to 2
      (cm7_core0 -> channel 0, cm7_core1 -> channel 1).

config MUX_SLOTS_PAYLOAD_MAX
    int "Max payload bytes carried in one slot"
    depends on MCUX_COMPONENT_component.serial_mux.slots
    default 240
    help
      No prefix in generated macro
      type unsigned
      Largest payload, in bytes, that a single shared-memory slot can hold.
      This is the capacity of the per-slot data[] buffer in the SPSC ring;
      a payload longer than this is rejected by the slot layer. Safe
      default (240): the slot header (2 x uint32_t) plus a 240-byte payload
      is 248 bytes, which pads cleanly to 256 (8 x 32-byte cache lines).

      Relationship to SERIAL_MUX_MAX_FRAME: SERIAL_MUX_MAX_FRAME bounds the
      largest DECODED COBS frame on the wire (channel byte + payload), while
      MUX_SLOTS_PAYLOAD_MAX bounds the PAYLOAD moved through one slot between
      cores. The hub strips the 1-byte channel prefix before dropping the
      payload into a slot, so the two are related by one framing byte:
      keep MUX_SLOTS_PAYLOAD_MAX <= SERIAL_MUX_MAX_FRAME - 1 so any decoded
      frame the hub accepts always fits in a single slot. With the defaults
      (240 and 256) this holds with margin (240 <= 255).

config MUX_SLOTS_DEPTH
    int "SPSC ring depth per direction per core"
    depends on MCUX_COMPONENT_component.serial_mux.slots
    default 4
    help
      No prefix in generated macro
      type unsigned
      Slots per ring per direction. MUST be a power of two so the head/
      tail counters wrap cleanly with a bit mask. Depth 1 is the original
      single-slot scheme; a small depth (4/8) lets short bursts through.
      Safe default (4): deep enough to absorb short bursts, still small.

config MUX_SLOTS_CACHELINE
    int "Cache line size (bytes) for counter/slot padding"
    depends on MCUX_COMPONENT_component.serial_mux.slots
    default 32
    help
      No prefix in generated macro
      type unsigned
      D-cache line size used to keep the producer-owned 'tail' and the
      consumer-owned 'head' on separate cache lines on the non-coherent
      shared bus. Safe default (32), which covers both the Cortex-M7 and
      Cortex-M33 lines; raise it only if a core has a larger cache line.

config MUX_REGION_ADDR
    hex "Shared-memory slot region base address"
    depends on MCUX_COMPONENT_component.serial_mux.slots
    help
      No prefix in generated macro
      Base address of the shared-memory region that holds the SPSC slot
      rings. Board specific and mandatory: it must resolve to the same
      physical memory on every core (hub and clients) and be reserved from
      all their linker maps. There is deliberately NO default: leaving it
      unset emits no MUX_REGION_ADDR macro, and the source then fails to
      compile (#error) so a missing region address can never be silently
      wrong. On imx943evk set it to cm33_core1 system OCRAM, 0x20800000.

config MUX_UART_TX_RING_SIZE
    int "Hub UART transport TX ring size (bytes)"
    depends on MCUX_COMPONENT_component.serial_mux.transport
    default 1024
    help
      No prefix in generated macro
      type unsigned
      Software TX ring buffer size, in bytes, for the hub UART transport.
      This is a tunable with a safe default (1024): deepen it only if a
      demo bursts more outbound bytes than the default ring can hold
      before the UART drains them.

config MUX_UART_RX_RING_SIZE
    int "Hub UART transport RX ring size (bytes)"
    depends on MCUX_COMPONENT_component.serial_mux.transport
    default 512
    help
      No prefix in generated macro
      type unsigned
      Software RX ring buffer size, in bytes, for the hub UART transport.
      This is a tunable with a safe default (512): deepen it only if the
      hub cannot service inbound UART bytes fast enough at the configured
      baud rate and the default ring overruns.

config MUX_HUB_DEDICATED_CONSOLE
    bool "Hub core uses its own dedicated physical UART for its console"
    depends on MCUX_COMPONENT_component.serial_mux.transport
    default n
    help
      Default (n): the hub core (which owns the mux UART) sends its OWN
      console output as one more logical COBS channel on the SAME physical
      mux UART, alongside the client cores. Every core shares one wire and
      one physical UART.

      When enabled (y): the hub keeps a SECOND, dedicated physical UART for
      its own console (standard DbgConsole/PRINTF), while the mux UART carries
      ONLY the client cores' virtual channels. Use this when the hub core has
      its own separate debug port. The board hub app.h must then also define
      the dedicated console UART wiring (instance index, baud, clock); on
      imx943evk cm33_core1 that second UART is LPUART11.

config SERIAL_MUX_MAX_FRAME
    int "Maximum decoded frame size (bytes)"
    depends on MCUX_COMPONENT_component.serial_mux
    default 256
    help
      No prefix in generated macro
      type unsigned
      Largest decoded COBS frame (channel byte + payload) the mux handles
      at once, in bytes. This is a tunable with a safe default (256):
      raise it if a channel needs larger single-frame payloads, at the
      cost of larger per-instance reassembly buffers.

endif # MCUX_COMPONENT_component.serial_mux

endmenu
